{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540084303,"sessionID":"ses_e2d148971ffeHy8aAsBGEu3eck","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2ec9926001SN07EO1LxR4nSw","sessionID":"ses_e2d148971ffeHy8aAsBGEu3eck","messageID":"msg_1d2ec6777001NTWFUcY0mWVahv","type":"tool","id":"call_cc303d9253494a3d93357355","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/README.md, lines 1-57\n1: ---\n2: title: create-readme Reference Map\n3: description: Overflow-only route map for README authoring, grouped into readme/ single-concern references.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme reference map\"\n6:   - \"readme creation standards\"\n7:   - \"create-readme references\"\n8: importance_tier: normal\n9: contextType: implementation\n10: version: 1.1.0.7\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # create-readme Reference Map\n14: \n15: Overflow route map for the `create-readme` packet. The complete authoring workflows live in [`../SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md); this set holds only the deep detail those workflows point to.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: `SKILL.md` is primary and self-sufficient: it carries the full README authoring workflow (Section 4) and the general and code-folder output shapes (Sections 5 and 6). Open a file below only when a task needs the extra depth, and load the single file that matches the concern rather than a large monolith.\n22: \n23: The overflow sits in one group: `readme/` holds README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail.\n24: \n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## 2. REFERENCE MAP\n28: \n29: Load the file that matches the current task.\n30: \n31: | Concern | Reference | Load When |\n32: | --- | --- | --- |\n33: | **README types and voice**: the five README types, progressive-disclosure stages and two-tier voice with worked examples | [readme/types-and-voice.md](readme/types-and-voice.md) | Deciding which README type to write, or how narrative and reference voice should split inside one file |\n34: | **README writing patterns**: per-section writing tips, heading hierarchy, analogy patterns, recommended table patterns, code-block and placeholder conventions | [readme/writing-patterns.md](readme/writing-patterns.md) | Drafting individual README sections, or reaching for a proven table or formatting pattern |\n35: | **README quality and checklist**, DQI weighting, per-section quality checks and the full pre-publish checklist | [readme/quality-and-checklist.md](readme/quality-and-checklist.md) | Reviewing a drafted README before delivery |\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 3. RELATED RESOURCES\n40: \n41: ### Overflow Subfolder\n42: - [readme/types-and-voice.md](readme/types-and-voice.md) - README types, progressive disclosure and two-tier voice\n43: - [readme/writing-patterns.md](readme/writing-patterns.md) - Section writing tips and formatting patterns\n44: - [readme/quality-and-checklist.md](readme/quality-and-checklist.md) - README quality criteria and pre-publish checklist\n45: \n46: ### Templates\n47: - [readme-template.md](../assets/readme-template.md) - Fillable scaffold for project, skill, feature and component READMEs\n48: - [readme-code-template.md](../assets/readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold with diagram examples\n49: \n50: ### Shared Standards\n51: - [core-standards.md](../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Document formatting rules\n52: - [hvr-rules.md](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) - Human Voice Rules\n53: - [validation.md](../../shared/references/validation.md) - Quality scoring (DQI)\n54: \n55: ---\n56: \n57: *End of create-readme reference map: depth lives in the `readme/` group.*","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/README.md, lines 1-57\n1: ---\n2: title: create-readme Reference Map\n3: description: Overflow-only route map for README authoring, grouped into readme/ single-concern references.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme reference map\"\n6:   - \"readme creation standards\"\n7:   - \"create-readme references\"\n8: importance_tier: normal\n9: contextType: implementation\n10: version: 1.1.0.7\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # create-readme Reference Map\n14: \n15: Overflow route map for the `create-readme` packet. The complete authoring workflows live in [`../SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md); this set holds only the deep detail those workflows point to.\n16: \n17: ---\n18: \n19: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n20: \n21: `SKILL.md` is primary and self-sufficient: it carries the full README authoring workflow (Section 4) and the general and code-folder output shapes (Sections 5 and 6). Open a file below only when a task needs the extra depth, and load the single file that matches the concern rather than a large monolith.\n22: \n23: The overflow sits in one group: `readme/` holds README type, voice, writing-pattern and quality detail.\n24: \n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## 2. REFERENCE MAP\n28: \n29: Load the file that matches the current task.\n30: \n31: | Concern | Reference | Load When |\n32: | --- | --- | --- |\n33: | **README types and voice**: the five README types, progressive-disclosure stages and two-tier voice with worked examples | [readme/types-and-voice.md](readme/types-and-voice.md) | Deciding which README type to write, or how narrative and reference voice should split inside one file |\n34: | **README writing patterns**: per-section writing tips, heading hierarchy, analogy patterns, recommended table patterns, code-block and placeholder conventions | [readme/writing-patterns.md](readme/writing-patterns.md) | Drafting individual README sections, or reaching for a proven table or formatting pattern |\n35: | **README quality and checklist**, DQI weighting, per-section quality checks and the full pre-publish checklist | [readme/quality-and-checklist.md](readme/quality-and-checklist.md) | Reviewing a drafted README before delivery |\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 3. RELATED RESOURCES\n40: \n41: ### Overflow Subfolder\n42: - [readme/types-and-voice.md](readme/types-and-voice.md) - README types, progressive disclosure and two-tier voice\n43: - [readme/writing-patterns.md](readme/writing-patterns.md) - Section writing tips and formatting patterns\n44: - [readme/quality-and-checklist.md](readme/quality-and-checklist.md) - README quality criteria and pre-publish checklist\n45: \n46: ### Templates\n47: - [readme-template.md](../assets/readme-template.md) - Fillable scaffold for project, skill, feature and component READMEs\n48: - [readme-code-template.md](../assets/readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold with diagram examples\n49: \n50: ### Shared Standards\n51: - [core-standards.md](../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Document formatting rules\n52: - [hvr-rules.md](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) - Human Voice Rules\n53: - [validation.md](../../shared/references/validation.md) - Quality scoring (DQI)\n54: \n55: ---\n56: \n57: *End of create-readme reference map: depth lives in the `readme/` group.*"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/references/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540083494,"end":1794540084303}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794540084320,"sessionID":"ses_e2d148971ffeHy8aAsBGEu3eck","part":{"partID":"prt_1d2ec9ac4001ojG3tBtqaFofJg","sessionID":"ses_e2d148971ffeHy8aAsBGEu3eck","messageID":"msg_1d2ec6777001NTWFUcY0mWVahv","type":"tool","id":"call_b04b3d4bcdfc4995b0228778","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md, lines 1-329\n1: ---\n2: title: README Template and Selection Guide\n3: description: Concise guidance for project, skill, feature and component README files, with routing to the code-folder README template when developer topology is the main need.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme selection guide\"\n6:   - \"project readme template\"\n7:   - \"skill readme routing\"\n8:   - \"narrative readme structure\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.2.3.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # README Template and Selection Guide\n15: \n16: Use this template for project, skill, feature and component READMEs. Use [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) for source-folder READMEs that explain package topology, files, boundaries and data or control flow.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. README SELECTION\n21: \n22: README files answer two questions: what is this, and how does someone use or navigate it?\n23: \n24: | Type | Use When | Template |\n25: |---|---|---|\n26: | Project | The repository or package needs a public entry point | This file |\n27: | Skill | A skill needs human-facing context beyond `SKILL.md` | This file |\n28: | Feature | A user-facing feature needs setup, usage or behavior notes | This file |\n29: | Component | A reusable module has an API, examples or configuration | This file, trimmed |\n30: | Code Folder | A source directory needs developer orientation | [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) |\n31: \n32: Create a README when someone can land in the folder and needs orientation. Skip it when inline comments or a nearby parent README already explain the folder.\n33: \n34: ```text\n35: Is this a project, package, skill or feature root?\n36: +-- Yes: write a general README.\n37: `-- No: is this source-code orientation?\n38:     +-- Yes: use readme_code_template.md.\n39:     `-- No: write a focused README only if readers need setup, usage or navigation help.\n40: ```\n41: \n42: Keep READMEs current-state only. Do not cite spec packet numbers, phase IDs or migration history. Use feature names, file paths, commands and stable source links.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 2. GENERAL STRUCTURE\n47: \n48: Use only the sections that fit the audience. Project and skill READMEs often need most sections. Feature and component READMEs should stay shorter.\n49: \n50: | Section | Include When | Content |\n51: |---|---|---|\n52: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n53: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes, limits |\n54: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n55: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n56: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n57: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n58: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n59: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n60: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n61: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n62: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n63: | Related Resources | Always when links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n64: \n65: Code-folder READMEs are different. Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands or known reader problems. Use [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 3. SKILL AND PROJECT PROFILE\n70: \n71: Use this profile for OpenCode skills, package roots and project-level READMEs that need more than a bare overview.\n72: \n73: | Profile Block | Good For | Keep It Short By |\n74: |---|---|---|\n75: | `trigger_phrases` | Search, memory and skill routing | 3-8 concrete phrases |\n76: | Key Statistics | Skill counts, tool counts, version facts | Use one compact table |\n77: | How This Compares | Neighboring skills or tools | Compare only reader-relevant differences |\n78: | Key Features | Current capability inventory | Group related features |\n79: | Requirements | Tooling and runtime needs | Separate required from optional |\n80: | Related Skills | Skill graph navigation | Group by relationship type |\n81: | Related Documents | Operator or developer references | Link stable docs, not packet history |\n82: | FAQ | Repeated questions | 3-6 high-value Q&A pairs |\n83: \n84: Sections use plain numbered H2 headings. Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` comments or a Table of Contents.\n85: \n86: ```markdown\n87: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n88: \n89: [Section content]\n90: \n91: ```\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 4. WRITING RULES\n96: \n97: Write for reading first, then scanning.\n98: \n99: - Lead with what the thing does.\n100: - Put the fastest useful path before detailed reference.\n101: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences. Keep bullets for commands, options, steps and parallel facts.\n102: - Use tables for options, file lists and comparisons.\n103: - When installing takes more than a few steps, keep only the minimum commands here and point to a root install document.\n104: - Give every inventory item a description. When an item needs more than one line, switch to the itemized pattern below instead of widening the table.\n105: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n106: - Test commands before documenting them.\n107: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n108: - Use relative links for local docs.\n109: - Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n110: \n111: The itemized pattern for inventories where each item needs more than one line:\n112: \n113: ```markdown\n114: **`spec.md`**\n115: - What the feature is and why it exists.\n116: - Required at every level.\n117: ```\n118: \n119: Group related items under an unnumbered `#####` heading when the inventory has natural groups, for example by how each item is triggered.\n120: \n121: Voice by README type:\n122: \n123: | Type | Voice |\n124: |---|---|\n125: | Project, Skill, Feature | Plain explanation first, reference detail later |\n126: | Component | Technical and concise |\n127: | Code Folder | Technical and concise, via `readme-code-template.md` |\n128: \n129: Human Voice Rules apply to all README content:\n130: \n131: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n132: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n133: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n134: - No setup phrases.\n135: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n136: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n137: \n138: Full reference: [`hvr-rules.md`](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md).\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## 5. FRONTMATTER\n143: \n144: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n145: \n146: ```yaml\n147: ---\n148: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n149: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n150: trigger_phrases:\n151:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n152:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n153: ---\n154: ```\n155: \n156: Use frontmatter when it improves routing, search or maintenance. Omit it for small human-only documents.\n157: \n158: ---\n159: \n160: ## 6. FILLABLE SCAFFOLD\n161: \n162: Copy this scaffold, then remove sections that do not apply.\n163: \n164: ````markdown\n165: ---\n166: title: \"[PROJECT_OR_FEATURE_NAME]\"\n167: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n168: trigger_phrases:\n169:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n170: version: 1.0.0.0\n171: ---\n172: \n173: # [PROJECT_OR_FEATURE_NAME]\n174: \n175: > [One-sentence purpose statement.]\n176: \n177: ---\n178: \n179: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n180: \n181: [Explain what this is, who it is for and why it exists.]\n182: \n183: ### Key Statistics\n184: \n185: | Metric | Value |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | Status | [Current state] |\n188: | Version | [Version or release channel] |\n189: | Main audience | [Who uses this] |\n190: | Operating modes | [Mode count or short list] |\n191: \n192: ### How This Compares\n193: \n194: | Capability | This Project | Related Option |\n195: |---|---|---|\n196: | [Capability] | [Current behavior] | [Difference] |\n197: \n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: ## 2. QUICK START\n202: \n203: ```bash\n204: # Install or prepare\n205: [command]\n206: \n207: # Verify\n208: [command]\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Expected result: [what success looks like].\n212: \n213: \n214: ---\n215: \n216: ## 3. FEATURES\n217: \n218: ### Key Features\n219: \n220: | Feature | What It Does |\n221: |---|---|\n222: | [Feature] | [Current behavior] |\n223: \n224: \n225: ---\n226: \n227: ## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n228: \n229: | Requirement | Minimum | Notes |\n230: |---|---|---|\n231: | [Runtime or tool] | [Version] | [Why it is needed] |\n232: \n233: \n234: ---\n235: \n236: ## 5. STRUCTURE\n237: \n238: ```text\n239: [root]/\n240: +-- [path]/        # [Purpose]\n241: `-- [file]         # [Purpose]\n242: ```\n243: \n244: | Path | Purpose |\n245: |---|---|\n246: | `[path]` | [Role] |\n247: \n248: <!-- For items needing more than one line, use the itemized pattern\n249:      from Section 4 instead of this table. -->\n250: \n251: \n252: ---\n253: \n254: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n255: \n256: | Option | Default | Purpose |\n257: |---|---|---|\n258: | `[OPTION]` | `[value]` | [What it controls] |\n259: \n260: \n261: ---\n262: \n263: ## 7. USAGE EXAMPLES\n264: \n265: ```bash\n266: [command]\n267: ```\n268: \n269: Result: [expected output or behavior].\n270: \n271: \n272: ---\n273: \n274: ## 8. TROUBLESHOOTING\n275: \n276: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n277: |---|---|---|\n278: | [Symptom] | [Cause] | [Action] |\n279: \n280: \n281: ---\n282: \n283: ## 9. FAQ\n284: \n285: **Q: [Question readers ask often]?**\n286: \n287: A: [Short answer with a link when useful.]\n288: \n289: \n290: ---\n291: \n292: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n293: \n294: ### Related Skills\n295: \n296: | Skill | Relationship | Use When |\n297: |---|---|---|\n298: | [`[skill-name]`](../skill-name/SKILL.md) | [Sibling or dependency] | [When to use it] |\n299: \n300: ### Related Documents\n301: \n302: | Document | Purpose |\n303: |---|---|\n304: | [`[Document]`](./path/to/doc.md) | [Why it matters] |\n305: \n306: ````\n307: \n308: ---\n309: \n310: ## 7. VALIDATION CHECKLIST\n311: \n312: - [ ] README type is correct. Code folders use `readme-code-template.md`.\n313: - [ ] Included sections match the audience and task.\n314: - [ ] No empty sections remain.\n315: - [ ] Commands were tested or marked as examples.\n316: - [ ] Links use correct relative paths.\n317: - [ ] Frontmatter is valid if present.\n318: - [ ] No Table of Contents and no `<!-- ANCHOR -->` comments.\n319: - [ ] HVR passes: no em dashes, semicolons, banned words or setup phrases.\n320: \n321: ---\n322: \n323: ## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n324: \n325: - [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold.\n326: - [`SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md) - README workflow and reference map.\n327: - [`core-standards.md`](../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Markdown formatting rules.\n328: - [`validation.md`](../../shared/references/validation.md) - Document validation and DQI scoring.\n329: - [`hvr-rules.md`](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) - Human Voice Rules.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md, lines 1-329\n1: ---\n2: title: README Template and Selection Guide\n3: description: Concise guidance for project, skill, feature and component README files, with routing to the code-folder README template when developer topology is the main need.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"readme selection guide\"\n6:   - \"project readme template\"\n7:   - \"skill readme routing\"\n8:   - \"narrative readme structure\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 1.2.3.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # README Template and Selection Guide\n15: \n16: Use this template for project, skill, feature and component READMEs. Use [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) for source-folder READMEs that explain package topology, files, boundaries and data or control flow.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. README SELECTION\n21: \n22: README files answer two questions: what is this, and how does someone use or navigate it?\n23: \n24: | Type | Use When | Template |\n25: |---|---|---|\n26: | Project | The repository or package needs a public entry point | This file |\n27: | Skill | A skill needs human-facing context beyond `SKILL.md` | This file |\n28: | Feature | A user-facing feature needs setup, usage or behavior notes | This file |\n29: | Component | A reusable module has an API, examples or configuration | This file, trimmed |\n30: | Code Folder | A source directory needs developer orientation | [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) |\n31: \n32: Create a README when someone can land in the folder and needs orientation. Skip it when inline comments or a nearby parent README already explain the folder.\n33: \n34: ```text\n35: Is this a project, package, skill or feature root?\n36: +-- Yes: write a general README.\n37: `-- No: is this source-code orientation?\n38:     +-- Yes: use readme_code_template.md.\n39:     `-- No: write a focused README only if readers need setup, usage or navigation help.\n40: ```\n41: \n42: Keep READMEs current-state only. Do not cite spec packet numbers, phase IDs or migration history. Use feature names, file paths, commands and stable source links.\n43: \n44: ---\n45: \n46: ## 2. GENERAL STRUCTURE\n47: \n48: Use only the sections that fit the audience. Project and skill READMEs often need most sections. Feature and component READMEs should stay shorter.\n49: \n50: | Section | Include When | Content |\n51: |---|---|---|\n52: | Overview | Always | Purpose, audience, main value and prerequisites |\n53: | Key Statistics | Useful facts help readers decide scope | Counts, version, supported modes, limits |\n54: | How This Compares | Readers may confuse this with nearby tools | Short comparison table |\n55: | Quick Start | Readers need to run or try something | Short setup, verification and first use |\n56: | Features | Capabilities need explanation | Feature groups, behavior notes and examples |\n57: | Requirements | Runtime, tool or service needs exist | Minimum versions, permissions and external services |\n58: | Structure | Navigation matters | Directory tree and key files |\n59: | Configuration | Settings exist | Config files, options, defaults and env vars |\n60: | Usage Examples | Patterns are easier shown than described | Simple and advanced examples with expected results |\n61: | Troubleshooting | Users can hit known issues | What they see, cause and fix |\n62: | FAQ | Repeated questions exist | Short answers only |\n63: | Related Resources | Always when links exist | Grouped skills, documents, commands or external references |\n64: \n65: Code-folder READMEs are different. Do not force Quick Start, Features, FAQ or Troubleshooting unless the directory has runnable commands or known reader problems. Use [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md).\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 3. SKILL AND PROJECT PROFILE\n70: \n71: Use this profile for OpenCode skills, package roots and project-level READMEs that need more than a bare overview.\n72: \n73: | Profile Block | Good For | Keep It Short By |\n74: |---|---|---|\n75: | `trigger_phrases` | Search, memory and skill routing | 3-8 concrete phrases |\n76: | Key Statistics | Skill counts, tool counts, version facts | Use one compact table |\n77: | How This Compares | Neighboring skills or tools | Compare only reader-relevant differences |\n78: | Key Features | Current capability inventory | Group related features |\n79: | Requirements | Tooling and runtime needs | Separate required from optional |\n80: | Related Skills | Skill graph navigation | Group by relationship type |\n81: | Related Documents | Operator or developer references | Link stable docs, not packet history |\n82: | FAQ | Repeated questions | 3-6 high-value Q&A pairs |\n83: \n84: Sections use plain numbered H2 headings. Do not add `<!-- ANCHOR -->` comments or a Table of Contents.\n85: \n86: ```markdown\n87: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n88: \n89: [Section content]\n90: \n91: ```\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 4. WRITING RULES\n96: \n97: Write for reading first, then scanning.\n98: \n99: - Lead with what the thing does.\n100: - Put the fastest useful path before detailed reference.\n101: - Explain in short paragraphs of short sentences. Keep bullets for commands, options, steps and parallel facts.\n102: - Use tables for options, file lists and comparisons.\n103: - When installing takes more than a few steps, keep only the minimum commands here and point to a root install document.\n104: - Give every inventory item a description. When an item needs more than one line, switch to the itemized pattern below instead of widening the table.\n105: - Use fenced code blocks with language tags.\n106: - Test commands before documenting them.\n107: - Show expected output for verification commands.\n108: - Use relative links for local docs.\n109: - Remove unused sections instead of leaving placeholders.\n110: \n111: The itemized pattern for inventories where each item needs more than one line:\n112: \n113: ```markdown\n114: **`spec.md`**\n115: - What the feature is and why it exists.\n116: - Required at every level.\n117: ```\n118: \n119: Group related items under an unnumbered `#####` heading when the inventory has natural groups, for example by how each item is triggered.\n120: \n121: Voice by README type:\n122: \n123: | Type | Voice |\n124: |---|---|\n125: | Project, Skill, Feature | Plain explanation first, reference detail later |\n126: | Component | Technical and concise |\n127: | Code Folder | Technical and concise, via `readme-code-template.md` |\n128: \n129: Human Voice Rules apply to all README content:\n130: \n131: - No em dashes. Use commas, periods or colons.\n132: - No semicolons. Split the sentence or use \"and\".\n133: - No Oxford comma in inline lists.\n134: - No setup phrases.\n135: - No banned words from the HVR reference.\n136: - Prefer active voice and direct verbs.\n137: \n138: Full reference: [`hvr-rules.md`](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md).\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## 5. FRONTMATTER\n143: \n144: Frontmatter is optional for normal project READMEs and recommended for skill or system documentation that should be discoverable.\n145: \n146: ```yaml\n147: ---\n148: title: \"[DOCUMENT_TITLE]\"\n149: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n150: trigger_phrases:\n151:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n152:   - \"[routing phrase]\"\n153: ---\n154: ```\n155: \n156: Use frontmatter when it improves routing, search or maintenance. Omit it for small human-only documents.\n157: \n158: ---\n159: \n160: ## 6. FILLABLE SCAFFOLD\n161: \n162: Copy this scaffold, then remove sections that do not apply.\n163: \n164: ````markdown\n165: ---\n166: title: \"[PROJECT_OR_FEATURE_NAME]\"\n167: description: \"[One-sentence description]\"\n168: trigger_phrases:\n169:   - \"[search phrase]\"\n170: version: 1.0.0.0\n171: ---\n172: \n173: # [PROJECT_OR_FEATURE_NAME]\n174: \n175: > [One-sentence purpose statement.]\n176: \n177: ---\n178: \n179: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n180: \n181: [Explain what this is, who it is for and why it exists.]\n182: \n183: ### Key Statistics\n184: \n185: | Metric | Value |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | Status | [Current state] |\n188: | Version | [Version or release channel] |\n189: | Main audience | [Who uses this] |\n190: | Operating modes | [Mode count or short list] |\n191: \n192: ### How This Compares\n193: \n194: | Capability | This Project | Related Option |\n195: |---|---|---|\n196: | [Capability] | [Current behavior] | [Difference] |\n197: \n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: ## 2. QUICK START\n202: \n203: ```bash\n204: # Install or prepare\n205: [command]\n206: \n207: # Verify\n208: [command]\n209: ```\n210: \n211: Expected result: [what success looks like].\n212: \n213: \n214: ---\n215: \n216: ## 3. FEATURES\n217: \n218: ### Key Features\n219: \n220: | Feature | What It Does |\n221: |---|---|\n222: | [Feature] | [Current behavior] |\n223: \n224: \n225: ---\n226: \n227: ## 4. REQUIREMENTS\n228: \n229: | Requirement | Minimum | Notes |\n230: |---|---|---|\n231: | [Runtime or tool] | [Version] | [Why it is needed] |\n232: \n233: \n234: ---\n235: \n236: ## 5. STRUCTURE\n237: \n238: ```text\n239: [root]/\n240: +-- [path]/        # [Purpose]\n241: `-- [file]         # [Purpose]\n242: ```\n243: \n244: | Path | Purpose |\n245: |---|---|\n246: | `[path]` | [Role] |\n247: \n248: <!-- For items needing more than one line, use the itemized pattern\n249:      from Section 4 instead of this table. -->\n250: \n251: \n252: ---\n253: \n254: ## 6. CONFIGURATION\n255: \n256: | Option | Default | Purpose |\n257: |---|---|---|\n258: | `[OPTION]` | `[value]` | [What it controls] |\n259: \n260: \n261: ---\n262: \n263: ## 7. USAGE EXAMPLES\n264: \n265: ```bash\n266: [command]\n267: ```\n268: \n269: Result: [expected output or behavior].\n270: \n271: \n272: ---\n273: \n274: ## 8. TROUBLESHOOTING\n275: \n276: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n277: |---|---|---|\n278: | [Symptom] | [Cause] | [Action] |\n279: \n280: \n281: ---\n282: \n283: ## 9. FAQ\n284: \n285: **Q: [Question readers ask often]?**\n286: \n287: A: [Short answer with a link when useful.]\n288: \n289: \n290: ---\n291: \n292: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n293: \n294: ### Related Skills\n295: \n296: | Skill | Relationship | Use When |\n297: |---|---|---|\n298: | [`[skill-name]`](../skill-name/SKILL.md) | [Sibling or dependency] | [When to use it] |\n299: \n300: ### Related Documents\n301: \n302: | Document | Purpose |\n303: |---|---|\n304: | [`[Document]`](./path/to/doc.md) | [Why it matters] |\n305: \n306: ````\n307: \n308: ---\n309: \n310: ## 7. VALIDATION CHECKLIST\n311: \n312: - [ ] README type is correct. Code folders use `readme-code-template.md`.\n313: - [ ] Included sections match the audience and task.\n314: - [ ] No empty sections remain.\n315: - [ ] Commands were tested or marked as examples.\n316: - [ ] Links use correct relative paths.\n317: - [ ] Frontmatter is valid if present.\n318: - [ ] No Table of Contents and no `<!-- ANCHOR -->` comments.\n319: - [ ] HVR passes: no em dashes, semicolons, banned words or setup phrases.\n320: \n321: ---\n322: \n323: ## 8. RELATED RESOURCES\n324: \n325: - [`readme-code-template.md`](./readme-code-template.md) - Code-folder README scaffold.\n326: - [`SKILL.md`](../SKILL.md) - README workflow and reference map.\n327: - [`core-standards.md`](../../shared/references/core-standards.md) - Markdown formatting rules.\n328: - [`validation.md`](../../shared/references/validation.md) - Document validation and DQI scoring.\n329: - [`hvr-rules.md`](../../sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md) - Human Voice Rules."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/assets/readme-template.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794540083908,"end":1794540084320}}}}
